/** CRS key-points review: approved batch 1, four syllabus-ordered lessons. */
window.CRS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"creation-covenants","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"CREATION, HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY, THE FALL AND GOD'S COVENANTS — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe opening chapters of Genesis answer important questions in the biblical account: Who created the world? Why do human beings have dignity? Why did people disobey God? How did God continue to relate to humanity? Read each story in its own setting rather than mixing up events.\n\n1. THE CREATION ACCOUNTS (Genesis 1–2)\nCreation means God's bringing the world into existence. Genesis 1 describes a structured sequence:\nDay 1 — light and the separation of day from night.\nDay 2 — the expanse separating waters.\nDay 3 — dry land, seas and vegetation.\nDay 4 — sun, moon and stars.\nDay 5 — creatures of the sea and birds.\nDay 6 — land animals and human beings.\nDay 7 — God rested, blessing and making the seventh day holy.\nGenesis 2 focuses on the human setting: the man, the garden of Eden, the command concerning the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, the animals and the formation of the woman. These chapters have different emphases; do not treat the order of every detail as a single identical list.\n\n2. HUMAN DIGNITY AND RESPONSIBILITY (Genesis 1:26–28; 2:15–24)\nBeing made in God's image gives human beings a special standing and responsibility; it does not mean that people are gods. Humans are instructed to be fruitful, exercise stewardship over creation and care for the garden. Genesis 2 describes the woman as a suitable partner, and the union of husband and wife. JAMB may ask about the responsibilities rather than only the creation day.\n\n3. DISOBEDIENCE AND ITS CONSEQUENCES (Genesis 3)\nThe serpent questioned God's command. The woman and man ate from the prohibited tree. They became ashamed, hid and shifted blame. The narrative describes consequences involving pain, difficult work, broken relationships and expulsion from Eden. The serpent is cursed; the ground is cursed because of the man. Distinguish what the text says about each person.\n\n4. CAIN, ABEL AND THE FLOOD (Genesis 4; 6–9)\nCain and Abel offered gifts to God. Cain killed Abel after becoming angry and jealous; God questioned Cain and judged him, yet placed a protective mark on him. In Noah's time, widespread wickedness led to the flood. Noah obeyed God's instructions to build the ark. After the flood, God made a covenant that a flood would not again destroy all flesh. The rainbow is its sign.\n\n5. ABRAHAM AND THE COVENANT (Genesis 12; 15; 17; 22)\nGod called Abram to leave his homeland, promising land, descendants and blessing to the nations. His trust was tested repeatedly. Circumcision was the sign of the covenant in Genesis 17. Abraham's willingness to offer Isaac in Genesis 22 illustrates obedience and faith; God provided a ram instead. Isaac is the covenant heir, while Ishmael also receives a promise of descendants.\n\n6. COMPARE THE COVENANTS\nNoah — promise concerning the earth and living creatures; sign: rainbow (Genesis 9).\nAbraham — descendants, land and blessing; sign: circumcision (Genesis 17).\nIsrael at Sinai — covenant relationship with commands and obligations (Exodus 19–20).\nDo not swap the signs or assume all three covenants were made with the same people.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — CREATION AND HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY\nThe first creation account (Genesis 1:1–2:3) gives an ordered description of creation and repeatedly says that what God made was good. Human beings, male and female, are created in God's image (Genesis 1:26–27). In this lesson, “image of God” points to human dignity and responsibility; it does not mean that God must have a human body. “Dominion” is authority to care responsibly for creation, not permission to destroy it.\nGenesis 2 concentrates on life in Eden. The man is formed from dust and placed in the garden to work and keep it. The command about the tree shows that human freedom includes responsibility. The woman is described as a suitable helper or partner. Do not confuse the different emphases of Genesis 1 and Genesis 2.\n\nTHE FALL — CAUSE, ACTION AND CONSEQUENCES (Genesis 3)\nThe serpent questions God's instruction; the woman and the man eat the forbidden fruit. Their disobedience is followed by shame, hiding, blame, hardship and exclusion from Eden. “The Fall” refers to humanity's movement from obedient fellowship with God into sin and its consequences. The text does not identify the fruit as an apple; a question claiming that it was an apple adds information not supplied by Genesis.\n\nWHAT A COVENANT MEANS\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving promises and responsibilities. God's covenant with Noah (Genesis 9) includes a promise not to destroy all life again by a flood; the rainbow is its sign. The call of Abram in Genesis 12 includes land, descendants and blessing for other families of the earth. In Genesis 17 circumcision is the covenant sign associated with Abraham and his descendants. A covenant sign is not the same as the covenant promise.\nAbraham's name was changed from Abram; Sarai's name became Sarah. Isaac is the promised son born to Sarah, whereas Ishmael is Abraham's son through Hagar. Isaac's sons are Esau and Jacob; Jacob is later named Israel. Learn this family sequence because JAMB questions often test relationships rather than the main story alone.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — HOW TO SOLVE A COVENANT QUESTION\nStep 1: Identify the person named in the question: Noah, Abraham or Moses.\nStep 2: Identify whether the question asks for a promise, a sign or a human responsibility.\nStep 3: Match the passage: rainbow—Noah; circumcision—Abraham; law at Sinai—Israel through Moses.\nStep 4: Reject an answer that belongs to a different covenant even if it is a true biblical statement.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What was the sign of God's covenant with Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 9:12–17 names the rainbow as the sign. Answer: The rainbow.\nExample (v): What does Genesis 1 teach about the value of human beings?\nSolution: Genesis 1:26–27 says that humans were created in God's image. Answer: Human beings possess God-given dignity and responsibility.\nExample (vi): Was the forbidden fruit specifically an apple?\nSolution: Genesis 3 speaks of the fruit of the forbidden tree but does not name its type. Answer: No; the Bible does not specify an apple.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nDo not mix the rainbow and circumcision. Do not identify the forbidden fruit by an unsupported name. Distinguish creation in God's image from the later disobedience in Eden. When asked for a consequence of sin, name the consequence in the passage rather than a general moral lesson.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): On which day were humans created in Genesis 1?\nSolution: Genesis 1:26–31 places humans on the sixth day. The seventh day is God's rest. Answer: Sixth day.\n\nExample (ii): What was the sign of God's covenant with Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 9:12–17 identifies the rainbow. Circumcision belongs to the Abrahamic covenant. Answer: Rainbow.\n\nExample (iii): What does God's image in human beings imply?\nSolution: The passage gives humanity dignity and responsibility for creation. It does not claim that humans have divine powers. Answer: Dignity and responsible stewardship.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — NOAH'S FLOOD (GENESIS 6–9)\nWhy the flood happened: Genesis 6:5–13 describes widespread human wickedness and violence. Noah found favour with God and was described as righteous in his generation. God told Noah to build an ark of gopher wood, coat it inside and outside with pitch, and make rooms in it. Its dimensions were 300 cubits long, 50 cubits wide and 30 cubits high (Genesis 6:14–16). It had three decks, a door in its side and an opening near the roof.\n\nWho entered the ark? Noah, his wife, his three sons—Shem, Ham and Japheth—and their wives: eight people (Genesis 7:7, 13; 1 Peter 3:20). Animals entered according to God's directions. Genesis 6:19–20 describes pairs of living creatures; Genesis 7:2–3 gives additional instructions for clean animals and birds. Do not assume that the same number of every kind of animal is given in both passages.\n\nTHE FLOOD TIMELINE — LEARN EACH NUMBER\n• Noah was 600 years old when the flood came (Genesis 7:6).\n• God gave a warning of seven more days before the rain (Genesis 7:4, 10).\n• The rain fell for 40 days and 40 nights (Genesis 7:12).\n• The waters prevailed upon the earth for 150 days (Genesis 7:24). This does NOT mean that rain fell for 150 days.\n• The ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat in the seventh month, on the seventeenth day (Genesis 8:4).\n• After the mountaintops became visible, Noah waited 40 days before opening the ark's window and sending out a raven (Genesis 8:5–7).\n• Noah then sent out a dove. It returned because it found no resting place. After seven more days, the dove returned with a freshly plucked olive leaf; after another seven days, it did not return (Genesis 8:8–12).\n• Noah left the ark when the earth was dry, after God instructed him to come out (Genesis 8:13–19). From the stated calendar dates, the narrative spans approximately a year, not merely 40 days.\nThe raven and dove are different birds with different outcomes. The olive leaf indicated that the waters had receded enough for vegetation to be accessible.\n\nWHAT NOAH DID AFTER THE FLOOD\nNoah built an altar and offered burnt offerings (Genesis 8:20). God promised that the regular cycles of seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, day and night would continue while the earth remains (Genesis 8:22). In Genesis 9 God established his covenant with Noah, his descendants and living creatures: never again would a flood destroy all flesh. The rainbow was the sign. The command concerning blood and respect for human life also appears in Genesis 9. The rainbow is a sign, not the ark's construction material.\n\nABRAHAM — GOD'S EXACT CALL AND PROMISES\nGenesis 12:1–3: God told Abram to leave his country, his kindred and his father's house for a land God would show him. God promised to make him a great nation, bless him, make his name great, make him a blessing, bless those who blessed him, curse the one who dishonoured him, and bless all the families of the earth through him. Abram was 75 years old when he departed from Haran (Genesis 12:4). His wife was Sarai (later Sarah), and Lot travelled with him.\nGenesis 15: Abram wondered how he could have an heir because he remained childless. God told him to look at the stars and said his descendants would be similarly numerous. Abram believed God. God also foretold that his descendants would be strangers in another land and would experience oppression for 400 years (Genesis 15:13). Distinguish this prophecy from the later description of Israel's stay in Egypt.\nGenesis 17: God changed Abram's name to Abraham and Sarai's to Sarah. He promised that Sarah would bear Isaac. Abraham was 99 when this covenant was reaffirmed; Sarah was 90 when Isaac was to be born, and Abraham was 100 when Isaac was born (Genesis 17:1, 17; 21:5). Circumcision was to be performed on male infants on the eighth day as the covenant sign. Ishmael was blessed with descendants, but the covenant was to be established through Isaac (Genesis 17:18–21).\nGenesis 18: Three visitors came to Abraham near the oaks of Mamre. Abraham showed hospitality, and Sarah heard that she would have a son. Sarah laughed at the promise; the child's name Isaac is connected with laughter. Abraham interceded for Sodom, asking whether it would be spared for decreasing numbers of righteous people, down to ten.\nGenesis 22: God tested Abraham by commanding him to offer Isaac in the land of Moriah. Abraham went with Isaac; at the decisive moment an angel stopped him, and a ram caught in a thicket was offered instead. The story tests obedience and trust; it does not say Isaac was killed.\n\nOTHER EARLY GENESIS DETAILS JAMB MAY TEST\nCain worked the ground; Abel kept sheep (Genesis 4:2). Abel's offering was from the firstborn of his flock. After Cain killed Abel, God asked, “Where is Abel your brother?” Cain replied with the question about being his brother's keeper. God marked Cain so that others would not kill him (Genesis 4:15).\nAt Babel (Genesis 11), people sought to build a city and tower to make a name for themselves. God confused their language and scattered them. This episode occurs before the call of Abram in the Genesis narrative.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — FACTS AND REASONING\nExample (vii): For how many days and nights did rain fall during Noah's flood?\nSolution: Genesis 7:12 specifically says 40 days and 40 nights. The 150 days in Genesis 7:24 refers to how long the waters prevailed. Answer: 40 days and 40 nights.\nExample (viii): How long did the floodwaters prevail?\nSolution: Genesis 7:24 gives 150 days. Answer: 150 days.\nExample (ix): What did God tell Abram to leave in Genesis 12?\nSolution: The command names his country, kindred and father's house, and directs him towards a land God would show him. Answer: His country, relatives and father's household.\nExample (x): At what age did Abraham leave Haran?\nSolution: Genesis 12:4 records that Abram was 75 years old. Answer: 75 years.\nExample (xi): What was Abraham's age when Isaac was born?\nSolution: Genesis 21:5 states that Abraham was 100. Answer: 100 years.\nExample (xii): What did the dove bring back to Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 8:11 identifies a freshly plucked olive leaf. Answer: An olive leaf.\nExample (xiii): What was the sign of God's covenant with Abraham?\nSolution: Genesis 17:11 identifies circumcision. The rainbow belongs to the covenant with Noah. Answer: Circumcision.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING — DO NOT JUST MEMORISE\nExplain the difference between 40 days of rain and 150 days of prevailing water. Name Noah's three sons. State three promises God made to Abram. Distinguish Genesis 12, Genesis 15, Genesis 17 and Genesis 22. Explain why the rainbow and circumcision cannot be interchanged.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the sixth and seventh days should not be confused. Compare Noah's and Abraham's covenant signs. Describe two consequences of disobedience in Genesis 3.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button below to attempt mapped JAMB questions, then explain why the distractors are wrong."},"old":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"Creation means God's bringing everything into existence. Genesis 1 presents an ordered creation, including land animals and human beings on the sixth day and God's rest on the seventh. Human beings are made in God's image: they reflect and represent him and receive responsibility for the earth. This is not a claim that people are gods or that God has the same physical shape as a person. It supports respect for every person's dignity, including people of a different tribe, social position or ability.\n\nGenesis 2 concentrates on the garden and human relationships. The river divides into Pishon, Gihon, Tigris (Hiddekel) and Euphrates. After the statement that the man needs a suitable helper, the narrative describes the animals being brought to him; the formation of the woman follows. The account ends with husband and wife becoming one flesh. Eve's name is linked with being the mother of all living. Do not confuse this with the earlier words “bone of my bones.”\n\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving commitments. God's covenant with Noah includes living creatures and the promise that a flood will not again destroy all flesh. Its sign is the rainbow. God's promises to Abraham include descendants, land and blessing reaching other nations. Circumcision is its sign. Abraham's obedience in leaving his familiar home is a major example of faith in Hebrews 11. God's promise concerning Ishmael recognises him as Abraham's offspring, while Isaac carries the particular covenant line.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Noah: rainbow; Abraham: circumcision; Sinai: law and covenant obedience.\n• The Sabbath ends the first creation sequence; marriage closes the second account.\n• Being made in God's image is likeness and responsibility, not physical appearance or human divinity.\n\nGenesis places the firmament, separating waters, on the second day and the sun, moon and stars on the fourth. Genesis 9 connects the prohibition of bloodshed with God's image in humanity. Noah obeyed God's instructions and entered the ark with his household under God's covenant. Genesis 11 names Terah, Abram, Sarai and Lot as leaving Ur; Haran was their stopping place. At Moriah Abraham named the place “The LORD will provide.” At Bethel Jacob promised a tenth of what God gave him. Keep these places, persons and covenant responses distinct.\n\nGenesis 6:3 names one hundred and twenty years; interpretations differ over whether this is a lifespan limit or a period before judgement. Genesis 1 calls the gathered waters seas and the dry land earth.\n\nGenesis 2 assigns garden cultivation and care. Genesis 3 explicitly curses the serpent and ground; the humans receive painful consequences and expulsion. Noah's ark floats as the waters rise.\n\nGenesis calls Noah's material gopher wood; the precise tree identification remains uncertain."}},{"id":"moses-exodus","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"MOSES, THE EXODUS AND GOD'S COVENANT WITH ISRAEL — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe Exodus tells how the Israelites were delivered from slavery in Egypt and formed into a covenant community. Follow the events in order: oppression, Moses' call, confrontation with Pharaoh, Passover, crossing the sea, wilderness and Sinai.\n\n1. MOSES' EARLY LIFE AND CALL (Exodus 1–4)\nPharaoh ordered the killing of Hebrew male infants. Moses' mother hid him, then placed him in a basket on the Nile; Pharaoh's daughter found him. After Moses fled Egypt to Midian, God called him from the burning bush at Horeb. God revealed the divine name and commissioned him to lead Israel. Moses objected that the people might not believe him and that he was not an effective speaker. God gave signs and appointed Aaron to speak with him.\n\n2. PHARAOH AND THE PLAGUES (Exodus 5–12)\nPharaoh resisted Moses' demand to let the Israelites go. The plagues demonstrated God's power and challenged Pharaoh's refusal. Remember the final plague: the death of the firstborn. The Passover instructions involved a lamb, blood on the doorposts and a meal eaten in readiness to depart. The blood marked Israelite households in the narrative. Passover commemorates deliverance; it is not the same event as the crossing of the sea.\n\n3. CROSSING THE SEA AND WILDERNESS PROVISION (Exodus 13–17)\nGod guided the people by a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night. At the sea, Moses stretched out his hand and the waters divided. After escaping Egypt, the Israelites faced shortages and complained. God provided manna and quail, and water in the wilderness. These stories test whether students can distinguish a crisis, the people's response and God's provision.\n\n4. THE SINAI COVENANT (Exodus 19–20; 24)\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving promises and responsibilities. At Sinai, God called Israel to be a treasured people and a holy nation if they kept the covenant. The Ten Commandments describe duties towards God and other people. Moses received the law; the people were called to obey. The Sabbath command concerns keeping a day holy, not the sign of the Noahic covenant.\n\n5. THE GOLDEN CALF (Exodus 32–34)\nWhile Moses was on the mountain, Aaron made a golden calf at the people's request. They worshipped it, violating the covenant. Moses interceded for them, and the narrative includes judgment and renewal. The central lesson is that covenant membership does not remove the obligation to obey God.\n\n6. LEADERSHIP, SPIES AND ENTRY INTO CANAAN (Numbers 13–14; Deuteronomy 34; Joshua 1–6)\nTwelve spies explored Canaan. Joshua and Caleb urged trust, while the others spread fear. The generation's refusal had consequences during the wilderness years. Moses saw the promised land but did not lead the people into it. Joshua succeeded Moses, led the crossing of the Jordan and directed the conquest of Jericho. Distinguish Moses' crossing of the sea from Joshua's crossing of the Jordan.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — FROM OPPRESSION TO DELIVERANCE\nThe Israelites were oppressed in Egypt after the period of Joseph. Pharaoh ordered harsh labour and measures against Hebrew male children. Moses' mother hid him and later placed him in a basket among the reeds; Pharaoh's daughter found him (Exodus 2). Moses fled to Midian after killing an Egyptian who was beating a Hebrew. At the burning bush on Horeb, God called Moses to lead Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 3). Moses' reluctance is important: he questioned his ability and speech, while God promised help and appointed Aaron to speak with him.\n\nTHE PLAGUES AND PASSOVER\nThe plagues challenged Pharaoh's refusal to release Israel. The final plague involved the death of the firstborn. Before it, Israelite households were instructed to mark their doorposts with lamb's blood and eat the Passover meal (Exodus 12). Passover remembers God's deliverance. The crossing of the sea came after the departure from Egypt; it was not the same event as Passover.\nSequence to remember: oppression → Moses' call → confrontation with Pharaoh → plagues → Passover → departure → crossing of the sea → wilderness journey → Sinai.\n\nWILDERNESS PROVISION AND COMPLAINTS\nThe people faced thirst and hunger. God provided manna and quail, and water was supplied in the wilderness. Manna was food provided during the journey; it was not one of the Egyptian plagues. When the people complained, Moses had to lead a community that was still learning trust and obedience.\n\nTHE SINAI COVENANT AND TEN COMMANDMENTS\nAt Mount Sinai, God entered a covenant relationship with Israel (Exodus 19–20). The commandments address loyalty to God, proper worship, family responsibility and conduct towards neighbours. The first commandment concerns having no other gods; the command against making idols concerns images for worship. Do not merge them simply because both concern worship.\nThe golden calf incident (Exodus 32) happened after the people had received God's instructions; it shows a breach of covenant. Aaron was involved in making the calf, while Moses interceded for the people.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — SEQUENCE QUESTIONS\nFirst underline the event in the question. Next ask whether it occurred in Egypt, during departure, in the wilderness or at Sinai. Only then select the person or location. A correct event placed in the wrong part of the journey is still a wrong answer.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Where did Moses encounter God in the burning bush?\nSolution: Exodus 3 places the encounter at Horeb, the mountain of God. Answer: Horeb.\nExample (v): Which event immediately prepared Israelite families for the final plague?\nSolution: Exodus 12 describes the Passover instructions, including the lamb's blood on the doorposts. Answer: The first Passover.\nExample (vi): Why was the golden calf a serious offence?\nSolution: Exodus 32 describes the people making and worshipping an image while bound to worship the LORD. Answer: It violated their covenant obligation to worship God alone.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nAaron was Moses' brother, not his father. The burning bush preceded the plagues. Passover preceded the sea crossing. Sinai is connected with covenant law, not the original call at the burning bush alone.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why was Aaron assigned to assist Moses?\nSolution: Moses objected that he was not an effective speaker (Exodus 4:10–16). God appointed Aaron as his spokesman. Answer: To speak on Moses' behalf.\n\nExample (ii): Which event immediately preceded Israel's departure from Egypt?\nSolution: The final plague was the death of the firstborn, and the Passover instructions accompanied it (Exodus 12). Answer: The final plague and Passover.\n\nExample (iii): Who succeeded Moses as Israel's leader?\nSolution: Deuteronomy 34 and Joshua 1 identify Joshua. Aaron was Moses' brother and priest, not his successor as national leader. Answer: Joshua.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — MOSES' FAMILY AND THE CALL\nMoses belonged to the tribe of Levi. His father was Amram and his mother Jochebed (Exodus 6:20); Aaron was his elder brother and Miriam his sister. His mother hid him for three months before placing him in a papyrus basket coated with bitumen and pitch among the reeds beside the Nile (Exodus 2:1–4). Pharaoh's daughter discovered him; Miriam arranged for the child's own mother to nurse him. The name Moses is connected in Exodus 2:10 with being drawn out of the water.\nAfter fleeing to Midian, Moses helped the daughters of the priest of Midian at a well and married Zipporah. Their son Gershom's name recalls Moses' life as a foreigner (Exodus 2:15–22). At Horeb God appeared in a flame from a bush that burned without being consumed. Moses was told to remove his sandals because he stood on holy ground. God identified himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and heard Israel's suffering. In Exodus 3:14 God said, “I AM WHO I AM.” Moses was to tell Pharaoh to release Israel to worship God. Moses' objections included his unworthiness, uncertainty about God's name, fear of unbelief and difficulty speaking. God supplied signs involving the staff, his hand and water, and appointed Aaron as spokesman (Exodus 4).\n\nTHE TEN PLAGUES — IN ORDER (EXODUS 7–12)\n1. Nile water turned to blood.\n2. Frogs.\n3. Gnats or lice (translation varies).\n4. Flies.\n5. Disease or pestilence on livestock.\n6. Boils.\n7. Hail.\n8. Locusts.\n9. Darkness for three days.\n10. Death of the firstborn.\nThe first nine plagues were not the Passover. The tenth plague was connected with the Passover instructions. Pharaoh repeatedly resisted, and the narrative emphasises the hardening of his heart. Do not confuse darkness, the ninth plague, with the final plague.\n\nPASSOVER — WHAT THE ISRAELITES WERE TOLD (EXODUS 12)\nEach household was to take a lamb without blemish; it was selected on the tenth day of the first month and slaughtered on the fourteenth day at twilight. Its blood was placed on the two doorposts and lintel. The meat was roasted and eaten with unleavened bread and bitter herbs. They were to eat ready for departure, with belt fastened, sandals on and staff in hand. No leaven was to be present during the associated Feast of Unleavened Bread. The blood marked the houses to be passed over during the judgement. Passover means God's deliverance, not that Moses himself passed through the sea.\n\nTHE SEA CROSSING AND THE JOURNEY\nGod guided Israel by cloud during the day and fire at night (Exodus 13:21–22). At the sea, Moses stretched out his hand and God drove back the waters; the Israelites crossed on dry ground (Exodus 14). Miriam led women with tambourines in celebration after the crossing (Exodus 15:20–21).\nAt Marah the water was bitter and was made drinkable (Exodus 15:23–25). At Elim there were twelve springs and seventy palm trees (Exodus 15:27). Manna appeared with the morning dew; people were told to gather enough for each day, but a double portion before the Sabbath (Exodus 16). At Rephidim water came from a rock when Moses struck it as instructed (Exodus 17:1–7). These are different locations and episodes.\n\nSINAI — GOD'S INSTRUCTIONS AND ISRAEL'S RESPONSE\nAt Sinai, God told Israel that if they obeyed his voice and kept his covenant, they would be his treasured possession, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation (Exodus 19:5–6). Moses prepared the people to meet God. The Ten Commandments (Exodus 20) include worship of God alone, no idols, proper use of God's name, keeping the Sabbath, honouring parents, and prohibitions of murder, adultery, stealing, false witness and coveting.\nIn Exodus 24 Moses read the Book of the Covenant; the people promised obedience. Moses used blood in the covenant ceremony. During Moses' absence on the mountain, the people demanded gods to go before them. Aaron collected gold earrings and fashioned a calf (Exodus 32). Moses broke the tablets when he saw the idolatry; later the covenant was renewed (Exodus 34).\n\nSPIES, WILDERNESS YEARS AND SUCCESSION\nNumbers 13 names twelve spies, one from each tribe, sent to inspect Canaan for 40 days. Ten brought a fearful report, while Joshua and Caleb urged confidence in God's promise (Numbers 13–14). The people's refusal led to the judgement of 40 years of wandering, corresponding to the 40 days of spying. Joshua and Caleb were the faithful exceptions among the adult generation. Moses later saw the land from Mount Nebo but died before entry (Deuteronomy 34). Joshua led the people across the Jordan, while priests carrying the ark stood in the riverbed (Joshua 3). At Jericho the people marched around the city once each day for six days and seven times on the seventh day before the walls fell (Joshua 6).\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — IMPORTANT NUMBERS\nExample (vii): Which plague immediately preceded the death of the firstborn?\nSolution: The ninth plague was darkness; the tenth was the death of the firstborn. Answer: Darkness.\nExample (viii): For how many days did the spies explore Canaan?\nSolution: Numbers 13:25 records 40 days. Answer: 40 days.\nExample (ix): What was the consequence of the people's refusal to enter Canaan?\nSolution: Numbers 14 connects their unbelief with 40 years of wandering. Answer: Forty years in the wilderness.\nExample (x): What did God instruct Moses to remove at the burning bush?\nSolution: Exodus 3:5 tells him to remove his sandals because the ground was holy. Answer: His sandals.\nExample (xi): Who led the Israelites across the River Jordan?\nSolution: Joshua 3 describes Joshua's leadership after Moses' death. Answer: Joshua.\nExample (xii): How many times did Israel march around Jericho on the seventh day?\nSolution: Joshua 6:15 states seven times. Answer: Seven times.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nName all ten plagues in order. Describe the Passover meal and its purpose. Distinguish Marah, Elim and Rephidim. Explain why the 40 days of spying and 40 years of wandering are different. Compare Moses' sea crossing with Joshua's Jordan crossing.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the purpose of Passover. List two responsibilities under the Sinai covenant. Explain why the crossing of the Red Sea and the crossing of the Jordan must not be confused.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to test the sequence of events and their biblical references."},"old":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"The Exodus is the account of God's deliverance of Israel from slavery in Egypt. At Moses' call, God gave signs because Moses feared the Israelites would not believe he had been sent. When the rod first became a snake, Moses fled; he took it by the tail only after God's instruction. At the sea, the Egyptians acknowledged that the LORD fought for Israel when their chariots were impeded. God avoided the shorter Philistine route lest seeing war make the people return to Egypt.\n\nIn the wilderness, food and water tested trust and obedience. Israel's complaints about food were heard, and God promised meat in the evening and bread in the morning. The people were to gather manna as commanded, rather than store extra in distrust. The special instructions before the Sabbath also required obedience. The Sabbath command concerns keeping the seventh day holy; its numerical position differs between Christian traditions, although the biblical command remains the same. Common Protestant lists number it fourth; Catholic and Lutheran lists usually number it third.\n\nAt Sinai, the people were consecrated and washed their garments. They were not told to touch the mountain: boundaries protected the holy meeting. In Exodus 24 they promised to do God's words; Moses recorded them, arranged sacrifices and confirmed the covenant with blood. After the golden calf, Moses interceded by appealing to God's promises to the patriarchs.\n\nKeep the two rock incidents distinct. Exodus 17 records a command to strike the rock at Horeb. Numbers 20 records a command to speak to the rock in the wilderness of Zin; Moses struck it instead. A familiar action can therefore be wrong in a different setting.\n\nBefore entry into Canaan, the spies assessed the land and its inhabitants.\n\nCommon trap: identify the particular episode and sequence before choosing a familiar name or action.\n\nThe Hebrew midwives spared boys because they feared God. Moses encountered God in a bush that burned without being consumed. Exodus 4 gives three signs: rod becoming a snake, diseased hand restored, and river water becoming blood; these are not the numbered plagues. Exodus 24 names oxen as the peace offerings. After the golden calf came a plague. On the Sabbath, disobedient gatherers found no manna; overnight hoarding on ordinary days produced worms. Numbers 20 excludes Moses from leading Israel into the land. \n\nMoses acts as mediator between God and the people. Pharaoh seeks his life after the Egyptian's killing, so he flees. The first manna comes in the wilderness of Sin, distinct from Zin. Shiphrah and Puah receive families because they fear God.\n\nA cloud guides by day and fire by night. Acts 7 connects Moses' defence of his Hebrew kin with attempted deliverance, without endorsing violence."}},{"id":"samuel-saul-david","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"SAMUEL, SAUL AND DAVID — LEADERSHIP, FAITH AND OBEDIENCE\n\nINTRODUCTION\nIsrael moved from the period of judges to kingship. This lesson explains why the people requested a king, how Saul became king, why his leadership failed and how David rose to prominence. JAMB often tests names, actions and consequences.\n\n1. SAMUEL'S CHILDHOOD AND CALL (1 Samuel 1–3)\nHannah prayed for a child and promised to dedicate him to God. Samuel served under Eli at Shiloh. When God called Samuel at night, he initially thought Eli was speaking. Eli taught him how to respond. Samuel's call shows attentiveness and obedience. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas; Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah. Do not mix them up.\n\n2. ISRAEL REQUESTS A KING (1 Samuel 8)\nThe elders complained that Samuel's sons acted dishonestly and requested a king like the surrounding nations. Samuel warned them about the demands a king would make, including military service, labour and taxation. The issue was not simply that kingship itself was always forbidden; the account also stresses Israel's rejection of God's kingship.\n\n3. SAUL'S APPOINTMENT AND EARLY LEADERSHIP (1 Samuel 9–11)\nSaul, son of Kish from Benjamin, was anointed by Samuel and publicly chosen. He gained support after delivering Jabesh-gilead from the Ammonites. Distinguish private anointing from public selection.\n\n4. SAUL'S DISOBEDIENCE (1 Samuel 13; 15)\nSaul offered sacrifice when Samuel had not arrived as expected. Later he failed to carry out the command concerning Amalek fully, sparing Agag and valuable livestock. Samuel taught that obedience is better than sacrifice. Saul's downfall is associated with disobedience, not merely his military weakness.\n\n5. DAVID'S ANOINTING AND GOLIATH (1 Samuel 16–17)\nGod sent Samuel to Jesse's family at Bethlehem. David, the youngest son, was chosen. The lesson stresses that God looks at the heart, not outward appearance. David confronted Goliath with a sling and stones, trusting God rather than relying on Saul's armour.\n\n6. DAVID, JONATHAN AND SAUL (1 Samuel 18–31)\nJonathan and David developed a close covenant friendship. Saul became jealous of David's popularity and repeatedly sought to kill him. David spared Saul when he had opportunities to harm him. This demonstrates restraint and respect for the king's position.\n\n7. DAVID AS KING: ACHIEVEMENTS AND FAILURES (2 Samuel 5–12)\nDavid united the tribes and made Jerusalem his capital. He brought the ark to Jerusalem. He also committed adultery with Bathsheba and arranged Uriah's death. Nathan confronted him with a parable; David acknowledged his sin and faced consequences. A leader's achievements do not excuse wrongdoing.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — THE TRANSITION TO KINGSHIP\nA judge in ancient Israel was a leader raised up to deliver or guide the people; this role was not identical to a modern courtroom judge. Samuel served as prophet, judge and leader. He heard God's call while serving under Eli (1 Samuel 3). Eli's sons behaved wickedly, and the people's later request for a king was influenced by Samuel's sons' misconduct and their wish to resemble surrounding nations (1 Samuel 8). Samuel warned that kings could demand labour, property and service from the people.\n\nSAUL'S APPOINTMENT AND FAILURES\nSaul, from the tribe of Benjamin, became Israel's first king. He was anointed by Samuel. In 1 Samuel 13, Saul offered a sacrifice when he should have waited for Samuel. In 1 Samuel 15, Saul failed to carry out the command concerning Amalek fully and then tried to justify his conduct. Samuel taught that obedience was better than sacrifice. These are two separate incidents; identify which chapter a question refers to.\n\nDAVID'S RISE AND LEADERSHIP\nDavid, son of Jesse of Bethlehem, was anointed by Samuel while Saul was still king (1 Samuel 16). David's victory over Goliath showed confidence in God rather than reliance on military equipment. Jonathan, Saul's son, formed a close friendship with David and protected him. David repeatedly refused to kill Saul when he had opportunities; this shows respect for the office of the LORD's anointed.\nAfter Saul's death, David first ruled Judah from Hebron and later ruled a united kingdom from Jerusalem. He brought the ark to Jerusalem. He wanted to build a temple, but the task was left to Solomon.\n\nDAVID'S SIN, REBUKE AND REPENTANCE\nDavid committed adultery with Bathsheba and arranged circumstances that led to Uriah's death (2 Samuel 11). Nathan confronted him through a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's lamb (2 Samuel 12). David admitted his wrongdoing. Repentance does not mean that the consequences of an action disappear automatically.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — WHO DID WHAT?\nSamuel: called in Eli's household, anointed Saul and David.\nSaul: first king, disobeyed concerning sacrifice and Amalek.\nDavid: defeated Goliath, ruled from Hebron and Jerusalem, repented after Nathan's rebuke.\nJonathan: Saul's son and David's loyal friend.\nNathan: prophet who confronted David over Bathsheba and Uriah.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Why did Israel ask Samuel for a king?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 8 the elders cited Samuel's ageing, his sons' misconduct and their desire for a king like other nations. Answer: To have a king who would govern them like the surrounding nations.\nExample (v): What did Samuel mean by “obedience is better than sacrifice”?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 15 Saul tried to excuse disobedience by referring to animals kept for sacrifice. Answer: Carrying out God's instruction matters more than using religious offerings to excuse wrongdoing.\nExample (vi): Which prophet confronted David after the Bathsheba incident?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 12 identifies Nathan. Answer: Nathan.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nSaul was Israel's first king; David was not. Samuel anointed David; Nathan later rebuked David. Jonathan was Saul's son, not David's son. Distinguish Saul's two major disobedience accounts.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why did Israel's elders request a king?\nSolution: Samuel's sons did not follow his example and accepted bribes; the elders wanted a king to judge and lead them like other nations (1 Samuel 8). Answer: Corrupt judges and the desire for a king like other nations.\n\nExample (ii): What did Samuel mean by obedience being better than sacrifice?\nSolution: Saul attempted to justify keeping livestock after disobeying God's instruction (1 Samuel 15). Religious offerings could not replace obedience. Answer: God's command must be obeyed rather than excused by ritual.\n\nExample (iii): Who confronted David over Bathsheba and Uriah?\nSolution: The prophet Nathan used a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's lamb (2 Samuel 12). Answer: Nathan.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — HANNAH AND SAMUEL (1 SAMUEL 1–3)\nElkanah had two wives, Hannah and Peninnah. Hannah was distressed because she had no child, while Peninnah provoked her. At Shiloh Hannah prayed silently and promised that if God gave her a son, she would dedicate him to the LORD for his whole life (1 Samuel 1:11). Eli initially thought she was drunk, but later blessed her. She named the child Samuel and brought him to serve at the sanctuary after he was weaned. Her song in 1 Samuel 2 praises God's power to reverse human circumstances.\nEli's sons Hophni and Phinehas misused their priestly position and treated offerings with contempt. God announced judgement on Eli's household. Samuel heard his name called at night and ran to Eli three times before Eli recognised that the LORD was calling him. Eli taught him to say, “Speak, LORD, for your servant hears” (1 Samuel 3). The message concerned judgement on Eli's household. Samuel became recognised as a prophet throughout Israel.\n\nTHE ARK AND ISRAEL'S CRISIS (1 SAMUEL 4–7)\nIsrael took the ark into battle against the Philistines as though its presence guaranteed victory. Israel was defeated, Hophni and Phinehas died, and the ark was captured. Eli died after hearing the news. The Philistines placed the ark in the temple of Dagon; their idol fell before it (1 Samuel 5). The ark later returned to Israel. Samuel called the people to put away foreign gods and return to the LORD. At Mizpah the people confessed their sins; Samuel interceded, and Israel defeated the Philistines (1 Samuel 7). He set up a stone called Ebenezer, meaning a stone of help.\n\nWHY ISRAEL WANTED A KING (1 SAMUEL 8)\nSamuel's sons Joel and Abijah were judges at Beersheba but accepted bribes and perverted justice. The elders asked for a king to judge them like other nations. Samuel warned that a king would take sons for military service, daughters for royal work, a tenth of produce and servants for his service. The people still insisted. God told Samuel to heed their demand while warning them about the consequences.\n\nSAUL'S FAMILY, ANOINTING AND REJECTION\nSaul was the son of Kish of the tribe of Benjamin (1 Samuel 9). He met Samuel while searching for his father's lost donkeys. Samuel privately anointed him (1 Samuel 10); he was later chosen publicly at Mizpah. Saul's rescue of Jabesh-gilead from Nahash the Ammonite strengthened his position (1 Samuel 11).\nIn 1 Samuel 13 Saul failed to wait for Samuel at Gilgal and offered the burnt offering. In 1 Samuel 15 Saul was commanded to destroy Amalek's possessions but spared King Agag and the best animals. He claimed the animals were kept for sacrifice. Samuel replied that obedience was better than sacrifice and rebellion was like divination. Samuel then killed Agag (1 Samuel 15:33). These were distinct offences; JAMB may ask which incident led to which rebuke.\n\nDAVID — ANOINTING, GOLIATH AND FRIENDSHIP\nSamuel went to Jesse's home in Bethlehem. Seven older sons were presented, but God chose David, who was tending sheep (1 Samuel 16). David later faced Goliath of Gath, a Philistine champion. He rejected Saul's armour, took his staff, sling and five smooth stones, and struck Goliath on the forehead with one stone (1 Samuel 17). David then used Goliath's sword to kill him. Five stones were selected; only one struck Goliath in the account.\nJonathan, Saul's son, made a covenant with David and gave him his robe, armour, sword, bow and belt (1 Samuel 18:1–4). Saul grew jealous when women sang that Saul had killed thousands but David tens of thousands. David spared Saul in the cave at En-gedi (1 Samuel 24) and again in the wilderness of Ziph (1 Samuel 26). In one episode he cut a corner of Saul's robe; in the other he took Saul's spear and water jar. These are separate events.\n\nDAVID'S KINGSHIP AND THE NATHAN INCIDENT\nDavid first reigned over Judah in Hebron for seven years and six months, then ruled over all Israel from Jerusalem for 33 years, making a total of 40 years (2 Samuel 5:4–5). He captured Jerusalem and brought the ark there (2 Samuel 6). God's promise concerning David's dynasty appears in 2 Samuel 7.\nDavid saw Bathsheba bathing, committed adultery with her, and arranged for Uriah to be placed in dangerous battle where he died (2 Samuel 11). Nathan used a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's only ewe lamb to expose David's sin (2 Samuel 12). David confessed. The child born from that union died; later Bathsheba bore Solomon. Do not confuse Nathan's rebuke with Samuel's rebuke of Saul.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — PEOPLE AND DETAILS\nExample (vii): What was Hannah's promise if she had a son?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 1:11 she promised to give him to the LORD all his life. Answer: To dedicate her son to God's service.\nExample (viii): What was Saul searching for when he first met Samuel?\nSolution: 1 Samuel 9 says he was searching for his father's missing donkeys. Answer: Lost donkeys.\nExample (ix): How many stones did David select before confronting Goliath?\nSolution: 1 Samuel 17:40 says five smooth stones. Answer: Five.\nExample (x): What did David take from Saul during the second occasion when he spared him?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 26 he took Saul's spear and water jar. Answer: His spear and water jar.\nExample (xi): How long did David reign in total?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 5:4–5 gives seven years and six months in Hebron plus 33 years in Jerusalem, conventionally stated as 40 years. Answer: Forty years.\nExample (xii): Which prophet used the parable of the poor man's lamb to rebuke David?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 12 identifies Nathan. Answer: Nathan.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nDistinguish Eli's sons from Samuel's sons. Explain why the ark's presence did not guarantee victory. Compare Saul's offences in 1 Samuel 13 and 15. Distinguish the robe episode from the spear-and-water-jar episode. Identify who anointed Saul and David and who rebuked David.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nName Eli's sons and Samuel's sons separately. Give two reasons Saul lost divine approval. Explain how David responded to Nathan's rebuke.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise names, events, causes and consequences."},"old":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"Samuel served God under Eli. Hannah had dedicated him in fulfilment of her vow; Eli later prayed that she would have more children. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas. Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah, who became judges but took bribes. When Samuel reported judgement on Eli's household, Eli acknowledged the LORD's authority to do what seemed good to him.\n\nIsrael's demand for a king marked a change from the earlier leadership associated with judges. Theocracy means rule under God's authority; it does not mean Israel had no human leaders. Saul's rescue of Jabesh-gilead brought public support. He refused revenge on earlier opponents because the LORD had brought deliverance. In the Amalekite campaign he spared what he was commanded to destroy and afterwards set up a monument to himself. The Kenites were warned to leave because they had shown Israel kindness.\n\nDavid first came to Saul's court to serve as a musician; fighting Goliath belongs later. Saul's promised reward for defeating Goliath included his daughter in marriage. Seven of Jesse's sons passed before Samuel before David was brought from tending sheep. David's hiding places include Adullam and Hachilah; the Ziphites reported his whereabouts to Saul.\n\nDavid refused Abishai's proposal to kill Saul. He would not incur guilt by striking the LORD's anointed and trusted God to determine Saul's end. At Ziklag, he sought God's direction before pursuing the Amalekites. His later kingship faced Absalom's revolt, which forced him to flee Jerusalem.\n\nSamuel's sons differ from Eli's; David's first summons concerns music. Trust never excuses dishonesty, pride or abuse.\n\nAn unnamed man of God warned Eli before Samuel received his message. Saul later sought a medium when threatened by the Philistines. David's spies confirmed Saul's arrival at Ziph. Israel pursued the Philistines after Goliath's fall. At Ziklag David consulted God through the priest and ephod. His servants feared he might harm himself after his child's death. David's repentance after confronting his own wrongdoing contrasts with Saul's self-justification. During Absalom's revolt a soldier respected David's order to spare his son; the victorious troops entered quietly because David mourned. The story does not excuse David's abuse of Uriah.\n\nAt Ashdod, the Philistines suffered tumours after bringing in the ark; possessing a sacred object did not guarantee blessing.\n\nSaul says he feared the people and obeyed their voice. Before consulting the medium, he receives no answer through dreams, Urim or prophets. Their wrongdoing, not merely Samuel's age, explains the complaint.\n\nEli warns his sons about sinning against God, but fails to restrain them. God announces judgement to Samuel over that failure. Saul's servant suggests consulting a seer about the lost donkeys. Amalek's attack after the Exodus explains the later command."}},{"id":"kings-division","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"SOLOMON, THE DIVIDED KINGDOM AND RELIGIOUS REFORMS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nAfter David, Solomon's reign brought great achievements but also problems. His successor Rehoboam faced a divided kingdom. Later kings and prophets challenged idolatry and injustice. Students should distinguish Israel (north) from Judah (south).\n\n1. SOLOMON'S WISDOM (1 Kings 3–4)\nAt Gibeon, Solomon asked God for wisdom to govern the people rather than for wealth or long life. The judgment between two women claiming one child illustrated his discernment. His wisdom became widely known, including to the queen of Sheba.\n\n2. THE TEMPLE (1 Kings 5–8)\nSolomon built the Temple in Jerusalem, with help from Hiram of Tyre, who supplied timber and craftsmen. The Temple became a centre of worship. At its dedication, Solomon prayed that God would hear the prayers of his people. David had prepared for the project, but Solomon built it.\n\n3. SOLOMON'S FAILURES (1 Kings 11)\nSolomon's many foreign wives turned his heart towards other gods. The narrative also describes substantial building and labour demands. His wealth and wisdom did not prevent spiritual compromise.\n\n4. WHY THE KINGDOM DIVIDED (1 Kings 12)\nAfter Solomon's death, the people asked Rehoboam to reduce the heavy burden imposed under his father. Older advisers recommended a gentle answer; younger advisers recommended a harsher response. Rehoboam followed the harsh advice. Ten northern tribes followed Jeroboam; Judah remained under Rehoboam, with Benjamin associated with the southern kingdom. The split also reflects earlier tensions and prophetic judgment.\n\n5. NORTH AND SOUTH: DO NOT CONFUSE THEM\nNorthern kingdom: Israel; first king Jeroboam I; important later capital Samaria.\nSouthern kingdom: Judah; first king Rehoboam; capital Jerusalem.\nJeroboam established worship centres with golden calves at Bethel and Dan to discourage travel to Jerusalem. Rehoboam was Solomon's son; Jeroboam was not.\n\n6. PROPHETS AND REFORM (1 Kings 17–19; 21; 2 Kings 22–23)\nElijah challenged Baal worship during Ahab's reign, including the contest at Mount Carmel. He condemned Ahab's injustice in the seizure of Naboth's vineyard. Much later, Josiah of Judah initiated reforms after the Book of the Law was found in the Temple during repairs. He removed idolatrous practices and renewed covenant commitment. Do not attribute Josiah's reform to Solomon or Jeroboam.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — SOLOMON'S ACHIEVEMENTS AND WEAKNESSES\nSolomon succeeded David and asked God for an understanding heart to judge the people (1 Kings 3). He became associated with wisdom, wealth, administration and the building of the Temple in Jerusalem (1 Kings 6–8). The Temple was a central place of worship, while the palace was the king's residence; they were not the same building. Solomon's many foreign marriages and involvement with other gods are presented as causes of religious unfaithfulness in 1 Kings 11.\n\nWHY THE KINGDOM DIVIDED (1 Kings 12)\nHeavy burdens under Solomon contributed to discontent. After Solomon died, the people asked Rehoboam to reduce their labour and taxes. The older advisers recommended a gentle response; younger advisers recommended a harsher one. Rehoboam chose the harsh answer. Ten northern tribes followed Jeroboam, while Rehoboam retained the southern kingdom centred on Judah. Jeroboam and Rehoboam are different men: Jeroboam became king of the north; Rehoboam was Solomon's son.\n\nNORTH AND SOUTH — A CLEAR COMPARISON\nNorthern kingdom: Israel; first king after division—Jeroboam; important later capital—Samaria.\nSouthern kingdom: Judah; first king after division—Rehoboam; capital—Jerusalem.\nJeroboam set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan to discourage travel to Jerusalem for worship. Bethel and Dan were northern worship centres, not two capitals of the southern kingdom.\n\nREFORMS AND THE BOOK OF THE LAW\nHezekiah of Judah is associated with religious reforms and trust in God during the Assyrian threat (2 Kings 18–20). Josiah's reforms followed the discovery of the Book of the Law during Temple repairs (2 Kings 22–23). Josiah renewed covenant commitment and removed idolatrous practices. Both kings were reformers, but the event involving the discovered Book of the Law points particularly to Josiah.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A DIVIDED-KINGDOM QUESTION\nAsk three questions: (1) Is this before or after Solomon's death? (2) Is the question about Israel in the north or Judah in the south? (3) Does it name the harsh response, golden calves or a later reform? These clues distinguish Rehoboam, Jeroboam and Josiah.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Who established golden calves at Bethel and Dan?\nSolution: 1 Kings 12:28–30 attributes this action to Jeroboam. Answer: Jeroboam.\nExample (v): Who succeeded Solomon as king?\nSolution: Rehoboam, Solomon's son, succeeded him before the kingdom split (1 Kings 12). Answer: Rehoboam.\nExample (vi): Which ruler's reforms followed the discovery of the Book of the Law?\nSolution: The account appears in 2 Kings 22–23. Answer: Josiah.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nJeroboam was not Solomon's son. Rehoboam's harsh reply helped trigger division; Jeroboam's calves encouraged false worship afterward. Solomon built the Temple; Josiah repaired and reformed worship in a later period.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What did Solomon request at Gibeon?\nSolution: He asked for an understanding heart to judge God's people (1 Kings 3). Answer: Wisdom to govern.\n\nExample (ii): What immediate decision helped cause the division under Rehoboam?\nSolution: He rejected the elders' advice and threatened heavier burdens (1 Kings 12). Answer: His harsh response to the people's request.\n\nExample (iii): Which king reformed worship after the Book of the Law was found?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22–23 names Josiah. The book was found during Temple repairs. Answer: Josiah.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — SOLOMON'S REIGN\nSolomon was David's son by Bathsheba. He succeeded David after a succession dispute involving Adonijah (1 Kings 1–2). At Gibeon God appeared to Solomon in a dream and invited him to ask for what he wanted. Solomon requested an understanding mind to govern and discern good from evil (1 Kings 3:5–12). Because he did not ask for long life, riches or the life of his enemies, God granted wisdom and also promised riches and honour. His judgement between two women who each claimed a living baby demonstrated his wisdom: he proposed dividing the child, and the true mother begged that the baby be spared.\nSolomon began building the Temple in the fourth year of his reign and completed it in seven years (1 Kings 6:1, 38). His palace complex took thirteen years (1 Kings 7:1). Hiram king of Tyre supplied cedar and cypress timber and skilled assistance (1 Kings 5). At the Temple dedication, the ark was brought into the Most Holy Place, and Solomon prayed for God's attention to the prayers of his people (1 Kings 8).\nThe queen of Sheba visited Solomon to test his wisdom with hard questions (1 Kings 10). Solomon's wealth, trade and building projects were impressive, but 1 Kings 11 reports that his foreign wives turned his heart towards other gods. The account gives 700 wives of royal birth and 300 concubines. His political and religious failures contributed to the later crisis.\n\nTHE DIVISION — PEOPLE, PLACES AND CAUSES\nThe prophet Ahijah of Shiloh met Jeroboam and tore a new garment into twelve pieces, telling Jeroboam to take ten as a sign of the northern tribes that would be given to him (1 Kings 11:29–31). Jeroboam had been an official under Solomon; he was not Solomon's son. After Solomon's death, Rehoboam went to Shechem, where the people asked him to lighten Solomon's heavy yoke. The elders advised him to serve the people and answer gently. The young advisers recommended a threatening reply. Rehoboam said he would make the yoke heavier. The northern tribes rejected his rule (1 Kings 12).\nJeroboam became ruler of the northern kingdom, Israel. Rehoboam remained ruler of Judah in the south, with Jerusalem as its capital. The north's later capital, Samaria, was established by Omri (1 Kings 16:24), not by Jeroboam at the moment of division.\n\nJEROBOAM'S GOLDEN CALVES\nJeroboam feared that if northern Israelites continued to worship in Jerusalem, their loyalty might return to Rehoboam. He therefore made two golden calves and placed one at Bethel and the other at Dan (1 Kings 12:26–30). He appointed priests outside the usual Levitical arrangement and established a festival. This is religious compromise intended to strengthen political control. The golden calves of Jeroboam are not the same event as Aaron's golden calf during Moses' lifetime.\n\nAHAB, JEZEBEL AND LATER KINGS\nAhab ruled the northern kingdom and married Jezebel, daughter of Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians (1 Kings 16:31). Their reign is associated with promotion of Baal worship and the injustice against Naboth. Elijah's contest on Carmel occurred during Ahab's reign. Omri, Ahab's father, purchased the hill on which Samaria was built (1 Kings 16:23–24).\nHezekiah was a reforming king of Judah who removed high places and trusted God during an Assyrian threat (2 Kings 18–19). Josiah was also a king of Judah. During repairs to the Temple, Hilkiah the high priest found the Book of the Law; Shaphan the secretary read it to Josiah (2 Kings 22). Josiah tore his clothes, consulted the prophetess Huldah and led a covenant renewal. He removed idolatrous objects and celebrated the Passover (2 Kings 23). The discovered Book of the Law and consultation with Huldah are distinctive clues to Josiah.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — SPECIFIC FACTS\nExample (vii): What did Solomon ask God for at Gibeon?\nSolution: In 1 Kings 3 he asked for an understanding heart to judge the people. Answer: Wisdom or an understanding heart.\nExample (viii): How long did Solomon take to build the Temple?\nSolution: 1 Kings 6:38 states seven years. Answer: Seven years.\nExample (ix): Which prophet tore a garment into twelve pieces?\nSolution: 1 Kings 11 identifies Ahijah of Shiloh. Answer: Ahijah.\nExample (x): Where did Rehoboam meet the people who asked for lighter burdens?\nSolution: 1 Kings 12:1 places the meeting at Shechem. Answer: Shechem.\nExample (xi): Who established Samaria as the northern kingdom's capital?\nSolution: 1 Kings 16:24 identifies Omri. Answer: Omri.\nExample (xii): Who discovered the Book of the Law during Josiah's reign?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22:8 names Hilkiah the high priest. Answer: Hilkiah.\nExample (xiii): Which prophetess was consulted during Josiah's reform?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22:14 identifies Huldah. Answer: Huldah.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nDistinguish Solomon, Rehoboam and Jeroboam. Explain why Rehoboam's reply caused division. Name the two locations of Jeroboam's calves. Explain how Ahijah's torn garment represented the split. Distinguish the reforms of Hezekiah and Josiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nCompare Jeroboam and Rehoboam. Explain why Solomon's reign is remembered for both achievement and failure. Name two actions associated with Josiah's reform.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise the divided kingdom, key kings and religious reforms."},"old":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"Solomon succeeded David and built the Temple; David's friend Hiram of Tyre provided materials. Solomon's organisation also involved heavy labour demands. When the people later asked Rehoboam to lighten their burden, they referred to this harsh service. Burdensome policies differ from religious offences.\n\nSolomon's turning towards other gods brought the announcement that much of the kingdom would be torn away. Division awaits his son's reign, retaining territory for David's sake. Rehoboam rejected the elders' advice to answer the people kindly and adopted the younger men's harsh counsel. First Kings 12:15 explains this as a turn of affairs fulfilling God's earlier word. Solomon's son Rehoboam differs from northern king Jeroboam.\n\nJeroboam established golden calves at Bethel and Dan. In later generations, royal misconduct affected the whole nation. Manasseh's shedding of innocent blood is specifically connected with judgement on Judah in 2 Kings 24. The judgement concerns injustice as well as false worship; religion never excuses violence against innocents.\n\nJosiah responded to the discovery of the law book by tearing his clothes and seeking the LORD. Huldah's reply says that his tender heart, humility and tears were the reason the announced disaster would not come during his lifetime. Do not substitute the general fact of his righteous reign for this particular reason.\n\nHis reforms included defiling Topheth to stop the sacrifice of children to Molech. At Bethel, he demolished the altar and high place and destroyed the idolatrous installation. Descriptions of breaking, burning and reducing to dust belong to the wider action; short multiple-choice options can oversimplify it.\n\nJosiah understood the book before seeking guidance; illiteracy was not the issue. Ceremonies never cancel justice.\n\nAsa removed ancestral idols. During Solomon's lifetime, Hadad became an adversary; the tearing away of the kingdom was assigned to his son's reign. Solomon's attempt to kill Jeroboam followed the threat to royal succession. At the Temple dedication Solomon recalled the promise of Davidic continuity with its condition of faithful obedience. Josiah deposed Judah's idolatrous priests; distinguish this from killing priests at the northern shrines. His reforms included Passover observance and sparing the tomb of the earlier prophet who foretold them. Jehoiachin surrendered when Babylon besieged Jerusalem.\n\nAt Gibeon, after a thousand offerings, God appears in a dream. Solomon's threatened division of a baby reveals maternal compassion. Josiah's workmen need no accounting because they act honestly. Asa burns Maacah's Asherah image at Kidron.\n\nJosiah dies fighting Neco at Megiddo. Solomon receives wisdom from God; at Gibeon, long life is conditional on obedience. Josiah renews the covenant after the book's discovery.\n\nSecond Kings 23 describes an exceptionally complete Passover, not proof that none had occurred since Judges. Ahijah foretells the division during Solomon's reign. Hilkiah finds the law book."}},{"id":"elijah-elisha","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"ELIJAH AND ELISHA — GOD'S POWER, TRUE WORSHIP AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA prophet is someone called to communicate God's message. Elijah and Elisha served in the northern kingdom of Israel, but their ministries were not identical. Elijah is especially remembered for confronting idolatry and injustice; Elisha continued the prophetic ministry through teaching, miracles and intervention in community life.\n\n1. WHY ELIJAH CONFRONTED AHAB (1 Kings 16–18)\nKing Ahab and Queen Jezebel promoted Baal worship. Baal was treated by his worshippers as a god associated with fertility and rain. Elijah's announcement of drought challenged the claim that Baal controlled the rain. At Mount Carmel, the prophets of Baal called without receiving an answer; Elijah prayed and the LORD answered with fire. The people were called to choose whom they would worship.\n\n2. DISTINGUISH THE LOCATIONS\nBrook Cherith — Elijah was fed by ravens during the drought (1 Kings 17).\nZarephath — a widow shared food with Elijah; her flour and oil did not run out, and her son was restored to life.\nMount Carmel — the contest concerning true worship took place (1 Kings 18).\nBrook Kishon — the captured prophets of Baal were taken there (1 Kings 18:40).\nUnder a broom tree — Elijah, fleeing Jezebel, expressed despair (1 Kings 19).\nMount Horeb — Elijah encountered God's presence and received renewed direction (1 Kings 19).\nThese are different events. Do not answer every Elijah location question with “Mount Carmel”.\n\n3. NABOTH'S VINEYARD: JUSTICE AND POWER (1 Kings 21)\nNaboth refused to surrender his inherited vineyard. Jezebel arranged false accusations against him; he was killed, and Ahab took possession. Elijah announced God's judgement. The lesson concerns abuse of authority, false testimony, greed and respect for another person's rights.\n\n4. ELIJAH AND ELISHA (2 Kings 2)\nElisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit. This expression is linked to the inheritance of a firstborn son; it is not simply a request for twice as many miracles. Elijah was taken up in a whirlwind, and Elisha continued the prophetic work. Elisha's ministry includes the healing of Naaman (2 Kings 5), the widow's oil (2 Kings 4), and the healing of Jericho's water (2 Kings 2).\n\n5. HOW TO ANSWER QUESTIONS ON THESE PROPHETS\nIdentify the person, the exact event, and the passage. For example, Naaman's healing is associated with Elisha, while the contest with Baal's prophets is associated with Elijah. Never use the general statement “both performed miracles” to ignore the event named in the question.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — WHY THE PROPHETS MATTER\nA prophet did not merely predict the future. Prophets taught the covenant, challenged rulers and helped people recognise God's authority. Elijah's message confronted both false worship and unjust government. Elisha's ministry showed God's care for ordinary families, foreigners and communities as well as kings.\n\nELIJAH'S FEAR AND RENEWED CALL (1 Kings 19)\nAfter the victory at Carmel, Elijah was threatened by Jezebel. His discouragement is not a contradiction of his earlier courage. God provided food and rest and later addressed him at Horeb. The narrative describes wind, earthquake and fire, followed by a quiet sound. The teaching point is that God's guidance is not restricted to spectacular displays. Elijah received further responsibilities rather than being abandoned.\n\nELISHA'S MIRACLES — UNDERSTAND THE NEED AND RESPONSE\nThe widow's oil (2 Kings 4:1–7) addressed a family's debt crisis: borrowed vessels were filled with oil, which could be sold to pay the debt. Naaman, an Aramean commander, was healed of a skin disease after following Elisha's instruction to wash seven times in the Jordan (2 Kings 5). Gehazi's dishonesty in the same chapter contrasts with Naaman's eventual obedience. In the story of the Shunammite woman (2 Kings 4), Elisha's ministry concerns hospitality, the birth of a son and the restoration of the child to life.\nWhen solving a miracle question, identify the need, the prophet, the instruction and the result.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A PROPHET IDENTIFICATION QUESTION\nStep 1: Underline the distinctive event: ravens, Carmel, widow's oil, Naaman or Naboth.\nStep 2: Ask whether the question concerns Elijah's confrontation or Elisha's continuing ministry.\nStep 3: Check the location and the biblical book before selecting an answer.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What was Naaman instructed to do to be healed?\nSolution: In 2 Kings 5, Elisha directed him to wash seven times in the River Jordan. Answer: Wash seven times in the Jordan.\nExample (v): What did the widow do with the oil provided through Elisha?\nSolution: In 2 Kings 4:7 she was told to sell the oil, pay her debt and live on what remained. Answer: Sell it to pay her debt and support her family.\nExample (vi): What happened to Elijah after Jezebel threatened him?\nSolution: He fled, became discouraged, received provision and travelled to Horeb (1 Kings 19). Answer: He fled and later received renewed direction from God.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nDo not assign Naaman's healing to Elijah. Do not confuse Elijah's stay at Cherith with his flight after Carmel. The contest occurred on Carmel; the captured Baal prophets were taken to Kishon.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where did Elijah challenge the prophets of Baal?\nSolution: Read 1 Kings 18. The contest was held at Mount Carmel. Answer: Mount Carmel.\n\nExample (ii): Why did Elijah condemn Ahab over Naboth's vineyard?\nSolution: Naboth was unjustly killed through a plot so that Ahab could take his inheritance (1 Kings 21). Answer: The abuse of royal power and injustice against Naboth.\n\nExample (iii): Which prophet instructed Naaman to wash in the Jordan?\nSolution: The account is in 2 Kings 5 and concerns Elisha. Answer: Elisha.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — ELIJAH'S PROPHECY AND THE DROUGHT (1 KINGS 17)\nElijah the Tishbite came from Tishbe in Gilead. He announced to Ahab that there would be neither dew nor rain except by his word (1 Kings 17:1). God told him to hide by the brook Cherith, east of the Jordan. Ravens brought bread and meat in the morning and evening, and he drank from the brook. When the brook dried up, God directed him to Zarephath in Sidon. There a widow was gathering sticks to prepare a last meal for herself and her son. Elijah asked for water and bread, and promised that her jar of flour and jug of oil would not run out until rain returned. Later her son became ill and died; Elijah prayed and the boy revived (1 Kings 17:17–24). Cherith and Zarephath are different places, and the widow's flour-and-oil miracle belongs to Elijah, not Elisha's widow's-oil miracle in 2 Kings 4.\n\nMOUNT CARMEL — THE EXACT ORDER (1 KINGS 18)\nIn the third year of the drought, God instructed Elijah to appear before Ahab and promised rain (1 Kings 18:1). Obadiah, an official in Ahab's palace who feared God, had hidden 100 prophets in two caves, 50 in each, and supplied them with food and water (1 Kings 18:4). Elijah asked Ahab to assemble Israel and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. The text names 450 prophets of Baal and 400 prophets of Asherah in the summons (1 Kings 18:19); the contest itself specifically describes the 450 prophets of Baal.\nTwo bulls were prepared on separate altars without lighting a fire. The prophets of Baal cried out from morning until midday and continued until the time of the evening offering, but received no answer. Elijah repaired the LORD's altar using 12 stones, representing Israel's tribes. He dug a trench and ordered water poured over the sacrifice three times, with four jars on each pouring. Fire from the LORD consumed the offering, wood, stones, dust and water. The people acknowledged the LORD as God. Elijah then had the prophets of Baal seized and taken to the brook Kishon, where they were killed (1 Kings 18:40). He prayed for rain: his servant looked toward the sea seven times and finally saw a small cloud like a man's hand; heavy rain followed (1 Kings 18:41–45).\nImportant distinctions: Mount Carmel = contest; Kishon = execution of Baal's prophets; seven times = servant's observation for rain; 12 stones = tribes; three pourings = water over the sacrifice.\n\nELIJAH AT HOREB AND THE CALL OF ELISHA (1 KINGS 19)\nJezebel threatened Elijah after Carmel. He fled to Beersheba and then into the wilderness, where he prayed to die. An angel provided food and water twice. Strengthened by the food, he travelled 40 days and 40 nights to Horeb. There came a great wind, an earthquake and fire, but the LORD was not described as being in these; a low whisper followed. God instructed Elijah to anoint Hazael over Syria, Jehu over Israel and Elisha as prophet in his place. Elijah found Elisha ploughing with 12 yoke of oxen and threw his mantle over him (1 Kings 19:19). Elisha said farewell to his parents, sacrificed his oxen and followed Elijah.\n\nNABOTH'S VINEYARD — THE LEGAL WRONG (1 KINGS 21)\nAhab wanted Naboth's vineyard near his palace in Jezreel, offering money or another vineyard. Naboth refused because it was his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel wrote letters in Ahab's name, arranged a fast and seated Naboth prominently. Two worthless men falsely accused Naboth of cursing God and the king; he was stoned to death. Ahab then went to possess the vineyard. Elijah pronounced judgement on Ahab's house and Jezebel. When Ahab humbled himself, the announced disaster on his house was postponed beyond his lifetime (1 Kings 21:27–29). The offence was not merely Ahab's desire for land: it involved false testimony, murder and unlawful seizure.\n\nELISHA'S SUCCESSION AND MIRACLES (2 KINGS 2–6)\nElijah and Elisha travelled from Gilgal to Bethel, Jericho and the Jordan (2 Kings 2). Elijah struck the Jordan with his mantle and the water parted. Elisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit; Elijah was taken up in a whirlwind, accompanied by a chariot and horses of fire. Elisha picked up the fallen mantle and parted the Jordan again. Fifty men of the sons of the prophets searched for Elijah for three days but did not find him. Elisha healed Jericho's water using salt in a new bowl (2 Kings 2:19–22).\nIn 2 Kings 4:1–7, a prophet's widow feared that a creditor would take her two sons as slaves. Elisha told her to borrow many empty vessels, shut the door and pour her small supply of oil into them. The oil stopped only when there were no more vessels; she sold it to pay her debts. In Shunem, a hospitable woman prepared a room for Elisha; her son later died and was restored to life (2 Kings 4:8–37).\nNaaman, commander of the Syrian army, suffered from a skin disease. A captive Israelite girl told his household about the prophet in Samaria. Elisha sent instructions for Naaman to wash in the Jordan seven times. At first Naaman was angry, mentioning the rivers Abana and Pharpar of Damascus, but his servants persuaded him to obey. He was healed. Elisha refused payment; Gehazi secretly obtained gifts and was punished with Naaman's disease (2 Kings 5). In 2 Kings 6, Elisha caused a borrowed iron axe-head to float.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — EVENTS, NUMBERS AND PLACES\nExample (vii): How many stones did Elijah use to repair the altar on Carmel?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:31 says 12, representing the tribes of Israel. Answer: Twelve.\nExample (viii): How many times did Elijah's servant look toward the sea before reporting a cloud?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:43–44 records seven observations. Answer: Seven.\nExample (ix): Who hid 100 prophets in two caves?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:4 identifies Obadiah, Ahab's palace official. Answer: Obadiah.\nExample (x): Where did Elijah travel for 40 days and nights?\nSolution: 1 Kings 19:8 says Horeb, the mountain of God. Answer: Horeb.\nExample (xi): What did Elisha ask Elijah for?\nSolution: 2 Kings 2:9 records a double portion of his spirit. Answer: A double portion of Elijah's spirit.\nExample (xii): Why was Gehazi punished?\nSolution: He lied to obtain payment from Naaman after Elisha had refused it (2 Kings 5:20–27). Answer: Dishonesty and greed.\nExample (xiii): How many times was Naaman told to wash in the Jordan?\nSolution: 2 Kings 5:10 specifies seven. Answer: Seven.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nRetell the Carmel contest in order, including the 12 stones and the water. Distinguish the 40-day journey to Horeb from the seven observations for rain. Explain the injustice to Naboth. Compare Elijah's widow at Zarephath with Elisha's widow who borrowed vessels. Name the rivers Naaman preferred to the Jordan.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the drought challenged Baal worship. Distinguish Mount Carmel from Brook Kishon. Give one example of injustice and one example of Elisha's ministry.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's Practice JAMB Past Questions button to test the events and their biblical settings.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nElijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."},"old":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"Elijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."}},{"id":"prophetic-justice","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"PROPHETS, TRUE WORSHIP AND SOCIAL JUSTICE — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nBiblical prophets were messengers of God. Their work included warning, teaching, correcting rulers and calling people to live faithfully. Social justice means treating people fairly, especially those who are vulnerable to exploitation. In the prophetic books, religious ceremonies without justice and obedience are strongly criticised.\n\n1. AMOS: JUSTICE MUST ACCOMPANY WORSHIP\nAmos preached against exploitation, dishonest trade, corrupt courts and comfortable people who ignored the suffering of the poor. In Amos 5:24, justice and righteousness are compared to flowing water. The image teaches that fairness should be continuous, not an occasional performance.\nAmos 2:6 — the righteous are sold for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals.\nAmos 4:1 — the “cows of Bashan” describe wealthy women in Samaria who oppress the poor.\nAmos 5:11 — people who trample the poor are warned about houses they will not enjoy.\nAmos 8:4–6 — dishonest trading practices and exploitation are condemned.\nAmos 8:11 — a famine of hearing the LORD's words is announced. This is not an ordinary shortage of food.\n\n2. HOSEA: COVENANT LOVE AND FAITHFULNESS\nHosea used marriage imagery to explain Israel's unfaithfulness and God's continuing call to return. Covenant love includes loyalty and mercy. Hosea 6:6 places steadfast love and knowledge of God above empty ritual. It does not mean worship is always wrong; it means ritual cannot replace faithful conduct.\n\n3. ISAIAH AND MICAH: RIGHT LIVING\nIsaiah 1:16–17 calls people to stop doing evil, seek justice, correct oppression and defend the vulnerable. Micah 6:8 summarises what God requires: do justice, love kindness and walk humbly with God. Learn the specific prophet and passage rather than treating every statement about justice as interchangeable.\n\n4. TRUE AND FALSE PROPHECY\nA prophet's message should not be judged merely by how pleasing it sounds. Jeremiah challenged false assurances of peace while wrongdoing continued. Students should distinguish a genuine warning that calls people to obedience from a flattering message that ignores injustice.\n\n5. EXAM TRAPS\nThe “cows of Bashan” are not the cattle offered in sacrifice; the phrase criticises oppressive luxury (Amos 4:1).\nThe famine in Amos 8:11 is a famine of hearing God's word.\nAmos, Hosea, Isaiah and Micah all address wrongdoing, but the wording and setting of each passage matter.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — WHAT MAKES WORSHIP GENUINE?\nWorship includes prayer, sacrifice and religious gatherings, but the prophets insist that outward actions must agree with daily behaviour. Someone who gives offerings while cheating customers or denying justice has not understood the covenant's moral demands. This does not mean that Amos or Isaiah rejected all worship; they rejected worship used to hide injustice.\n\nAMOS: IDENTIFY THE SPECIFIC WRONG\nDishonest scales and measures cheat buyers. Selling people for debts treats human beings as objects. Corrupt courts deny fair treatment to the poor. The “cows of Bashan” image condemns privileged people who benefit from oppression. These examples help explain why Amos demands justice that flows continuously rather than occasional acts of charity.\nA question about Amos 8:11 asks about a famine of hearing God's word. A question about Amos 8:4–6 asks about exploitation in trade. The chapter number alone is not enough; read the verse.\n\nHOSEA, ISAIAH AND MICAH — SIMILAR THEMES, DIFFERENT WORDS\nHosea's marriage imagery represents covenant unfaithfulness and continuing divine love. Isaiah 1 urges the people to learn to do good, seek justice and defend the vulnerable. Micah 6:8 summarises justice, mercy and humility. All three concern faithful living, but JAMB may ask which prophet used a particular expression.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A QUOTATION QUESTION\nStep 1: Read the key phrase rather than only the broad theme.\nStep 2: Match it to its prophet: “cows of Bashan”—Amos; “do justice, love kindness”—Micah; “learn to do good; seek justice”—Isaiah.\nStep 3: Check the action condemned or required.\nStep 4: Explain why a similar but differently worded option is less accurate.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Why did Amos condemn dishonest trading?\nSolution: It exploited vulnerable people and violated the demand for justice (Amos 8:4–6). Answer: It was unfair and oppressive.\nExample (v): Which prophet taught that people should do justice, love kindness and walk humbly with God?\nSolution: This wording is in Micah 6:8. Answer: Micah.\nExample (vi): Does Isaiah 1 teach that sacrifices alone guarantee God's approval?\nSolution: No. Isaiah calls people to stop wrongdoing and defend the oppressed. Answer: No; worship must be joined to just conduct.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nSocial justice is not simply giving money away; it includes fairness in courts, business and leadership. Do not mix Amos 8's dishonest trade with the famine of hearing God's word.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is the famine described in Amos 8:11?\nSolution: The verse identifies a famine of hearing the words of the LORD, not a lack of bread or water. Answer: A shortage of hearing God's word.\n\nExample (ii): What does Micah 6:8 require?\nSolution: The passage names justice, kindness and humble walking with God. Answer: Just conduct, mercy and humility before God.\n\nExample (iii): Who are the “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1?\nSolution: The prophet addresses privileged women of Samaria who oppress the poor. Answer: Wealthy, oppressive women of Samaria.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — AMOS: WHO HE WAS AND WHY HE SPOKE\nAmos was from Tekoa in Judah, but his messages concerned especially the northern kingdom of Israel. Amos 1:1 places his ministry in the days of Uzziah king of Judah and Jeroboam son of Joash king of Israel, two years before an earthquake. In Amos 7:14–15 he said he was neither a prophet nor a prophet's son by profession: he was a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs, and the LORD took him from following the flock. At Bethel, Amaziah the priest opposed him and told him to return to Judah (Amos 7:10–17). This is important because Amos was not simply an official court prophet delivering messages that pleased the king.\n\nAMOS' CHARGES AGAINST ISRAEL (AMOS 2, 4, 5 AND 8)\nAmos condemned selling the righteous for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals (2:6), mistreatment of the poor and abuse of vulnerable people (2:7), and exploitation through unfair taxes and corrupt justice (5:10–12). Amos 4:1 addresses the “cows of Bashan” on Mount Samaria: privileged women benefiting from oppression and demanding luxury. In Amos 8:4–6 merchants eagerly await the end of religious observances so they can sell grain dishonestly: they make the ephah small (giving less grain), the shekel great (charging more), and use false balances. Explain how each device cheats a buyer rather than merely memorising “dishonest scales.”\nAmos 5:21–24 says that God rejects festivals, assemblies and offerings when justice is absent. The positive demand is that justice should roll down like waters and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream. Amos 8:11 announces a famine not of bread or thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD. These two water-related images refer to different ideas: flowing justice and a famine of God's word.\n\nAMOS' VISIONS — WHAT EACH SYMBOL MEANS\nAmos 7:1–3: locusts threatened the land; Amos interceded and the LORD relented.\nAmos 7:4–6: judgement by fire; Amos again interceded and the LORD relented.\nAmos 7:7–9: a plumb line showed that Israel was being measured against God's standard and judgement would not simply be withdrawn.\nAmos 8:1–3: a basket of summer fruit signified that Israel's end was near. The Hebrew wordplay links ripe fruit with the end.\nAmos 9:1–4: a vision of the LORD beside the altar announced unavoidable judgement.\nA plumb line is a builder's tool for checking whether a wall is straight. It is not a measuring device for the amount of rain.\n\nHOSEA'S MARRIAGE IMAGE (HOSEA 1–3)\nHosea was instructed to marry Gomer, daughter of Diblaim. The marriage imagery represents the covenant relationship between God and an unfaithful Israel. The children are named Jezreel, Lo-ruhamah (“not pitied” or “no mercy”) and Lo-ammi (“not my people”). Their names convey messages of judgement; later passages offer hope of renewed relationship. Hosea 6:6 stresses steadfast love and knowledge of God over sacrifices offered without faithfulness. Hosea 11 depicts God's care for Israel using the image of a parent teaching a child to walk. Do not confuse Hosea's symbolic marriage with Jeremiah's new covenant.\n\nISAIAH, MICAH AND JEREMIAH — DISTINCTIVE TEACHINGS\nIsaiah 1:10–17 condemns worship that is accompanied by bloodshed and injustice, telling the people to wash, make themselves clean, learn to do good, seek justice, rescue the oppressed, defend the orphan and plead for the widow. Isaiah 6 describes his call: he saw the LORD enthroned, seraphim cried “Holy, holy, holy,” and a seraph touched his lips with a burning coal. Isaiah responded, “Here am I; send me.” The coal belongs to Isaiah's call, not Ezekiel's.\nMicah 6:6–8 asks whether offerings alone can please God, then answers that God requires justice, kindness and humble walking with him. Micah 3 condemns rulers who distort justice for gain. Jeremiah 7 warns that merely trusting the Temple while oppressing others is false security. In Jeremiah 26, opposition arose because of his warning concerning the Temple.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — QUOTATIONS AND SYMBOLS\nExample (vii): What occupation did Amos identify before his call?\nSolution: Amos 7:14–15 describes him as a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs. Answer: Herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs.\nExample (viii): Which priest opposed Amos at Bethel?\nSolution: Amos 7:10 names Amaziah. Answer: Amaziah.\nExample (ix): What did the plumb line in Amos 7 signify?\nSolution: It represented God's assessment of Israel and the coming judgement. Answer: God's standard of judgement.\nExample (x): What was Amos' fourth vision?\nSolution: Amos 8:1–3 describes a basket of summer fruit. Answer: A basket of summer fruit.\nExample (xi): Who was Hosea's wife?\nSolution: Hosea 1:3 names Gomer, daughter of Diblaim. Answer: Gomer.\nExample (xii): Which prophet's lips were touched with a burning coal?\nSolution: Isaiah 6:6–7 describes this event. Answer: Isaiah.\nExample (xiii): What did Amos mean by making the ephah small and shekel great?\nSolution: Traders reduced the quantity delivered while increasing the amount charged (Amos 8:5). Answer: Cheating buyers in trade.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nName Amos' five visions in order and explain the plumb line. Explain two ways dishonest traders exploited customers. Identify Hosea's three symbolic children. Distinguish Amos 5:24 from Amos 8:11. Explain why Isaiah rejected worship without justice.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain social justice in your own words. Name two practices condemned by Amos. Distinguish outward worship from covenant faithfulness.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked JAMB questions to practise identifying each prophet and the correct passage.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nA biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."},"old":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"A biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."}},{"id":"repentance-restoration","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"REPENTANCE, PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY AND RESTORATION — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nRepentance means turning away from wrongdoing and returning to God in thought and action. It is more than feeling sorry. Restoration means rebuilding a damaged relationship or community. The prophets taught that God's judgement is serious, but they also proclaimed opportunities for genuine change.\n\n1. HOSEA: RETURN TO THE LORD (Hosea 14)\nHosea calls Israel to confess sin, stop trusting foreign powers as saviours and abandon idols. His teaching joins honest words to a changed life. Hosea 14:9 explains that the LORD's ways are right: the upright walk in them, while rebels stumble. The difference is the response of the people, not a change in God's ways.\n\n2. JEREMIAH: CHANGE OF HEART AND A NEW COVENANT\nJeremiah criticised empty confidence in the Temple when people's behaviour contradicted their worship (Jeremiah 7). Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a new covenant: God's law written on hearts, knowledge of God and forgiveness. The promise concerns inner transformation, not merely replacing one building or ruler with another.\nJeremiah 3:15 describes shepherds who lead with knowledge and understanding. In this passage “shepherds” refers to leaders, not only people caring for animals.\n\n3. EZEKIEL: PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY (Ezekiel 18)\nEzekiel rejects the idea that an individual must automatically bear guilt for another person's wrongdoing. Each person is responsible for their own conduct. The wicked person who turns away from wrongdoing may live; a righteous person who turns to evil cannot rely on past good behaviour as an excuse.\nThe proverb about fathers eating sour grapes and children's teeth being set on edge expresses the attitude Ezekiel corrects.\n\n4. JONAH: REPENTANCE AT NINEVEH (Jonah 3–4)\nJonah announced judgement against Nineveh. Its people responded with fasting and turning from evil, and God did not bring the threatened destruction. Jonah was displeased by God's mercy. The account teaches both the seriousness of repentance and the breadth of divine compassion.\n\n5. HOW TO DISTINGUISH THE PASSAGES\nHosea 14 — return, confession and rejection of idols.\nJeremiah 31 — the new covenant and inward law.\nEzekiel 18 — individual responsibility and the possibility of change.\nJonah 3 — Nineveh's repentance.\nThese themes overlap, but the passages answer different examination questions.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — REPENTANCE IS A PROCESS\nRecognising wrongdoing is the beginning, not the end. Genuine repentance involves admitting the wrong, turning from it and choosing different conduct. Restoration describes the repair that follows: renewed trust, forgiveness and sometimes rebuilding damaged community life. A person may be forgiven and still need to repair harm caused to others.\n\nHOSEA: WHY TURNING BACK MATTERS\nHosea 14 asks the people to return to the LORD, speak honestly about their sin and abandon dependence on idols and military alliances. The chapter describes God's healing and renewed blessing. The message combines divine mercy with a real change in allegiance; it is not a promise that saying words without changing behaviour is enough.\n\nJEREMIAH'S NEW COVENANT — WHAT IS NEW?\nThe old covenant relationship had been repeatedly broken. Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a transformed relationship in which God's law is written on the heart. The passage emphasises inner knowledge of God and forgiveness. The new covenant is not merely a new political ruler, another Temple building or a different set of ceremonial objects.\n\nEZEKIEL 18 — FOLLOW THE ARGUMENT\nThe people quoted a proverb about parents eating sour grapes and children suffering the taste. Ezekiel challenges using inherited guilt as an excuse. He describes a righteous person, a wicked son and a righteous grandson to show that each person's conduct matters. A wicked person who turns away from wrongdoing may live; a person who abandons righteousness cannot rely on past good actions. The emphasis is responsibility and the possibility of genuine change.\n\nJONAH — REPENTANCE BEYOND ISRAEL\nNineveh was a foreign city. Its people responded to Jonah's warning, and the threatened destruction did not take place. Jonah's anger highlights a question about mercy: should compassion be limited to the prophet's own people? The book presents God's concern for people beyond Israel.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — IDENTIFY THE PROPHET\nIf the question names the sour-grapes proverb, think Ezekiel 18. If it names law written on hearts, think Jeremiah 31. If it names Nineveh and Jonah's displeasure, think Jonah 3–4. If it asks for Israel to return and renounce idols, think Hosea 14.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What did Ezekiel reject in the sour-grapes proverb?\nSolution: Ezekiel 18 challenges the idea that a person is automatically punished for another's wrongdoing regardless of their own actions. Answer: The denial of individual moral responsibility.\nExample (v): What does the law written on the heart signify?\nSolution: Jeremiah 31 describes inward knowledge and obedience rather than merely outward conformity. Answer: An inwardly transformed relationship with God.\nExample (vi): Why was Jonah unhappy after Nineveh repented?\nSolution: Jonah 4 shows his displeasure at God's mercy toward the city. Answer: He resented God's decision to spare Nineveh.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nRepentance is not identical to regret. Individual responsibility does not mean that people's actions have no effects on others. Jeremiah's new covenant and Ezekiel's personal responsibility are related but distinct teachings.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is central to the new covenant in Jeremiah 31?\nSolution: Jeremiah says God's law will be written on people's hearts and that God will forgive their sins. Answer: Inward obedience and forgiveness.\n\nExample (ii): According to Ezekiel 18, can a wicked person change their outcome?\nSolution: Yes. The chapter says that a wicked person who turns away from evil and does what is right may live. Answer: Yes, through genuine repentance.\n\nExample (iii): Why was Nineveh spared after Jonah's warning?\nSolution: Jonah 3 describes the people's response to the warning and their turning from evil. Answer: They repented.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — WHAT REPENTANCE REQUIRES\nRepentance involves recognition of wrongdoing, confession and a changed direction. Biblical prophets do not present it as merely feeling bad. In Hosea 14:1–3, Israel is told to return to the LORD, bring words of repentance, renounce reliance on Assyria and war horses, and stop calling handmade objects gods. Hosea 14:4–8 describes God's healing of their unfaithfulness and renewed growth through images of dew, lily, cedar and olive tree. The promise of restoration follows a call to turn away from false sources of security.\n\nJEREMIAH'S CALL AND TEMPLE SERMON\nJeremiah 1:4–10 says God knew Jeremiah before his birth and appointed him a prophet to the nations. Jeremiah protested that he was only a youth and did not know how to speak. God touched his mouth and promised to put words there. He was appointed to uproot and tear down, destroy and overthrow, build and plant. In Jeremiah 7:1–15, he stood at the gate of the LORD's house and challenged people who trusted the repeated phrase “the temple of the LORD” while stealing, murdering, committing adultery and worshipping other gods. The warning was not against the Temple itself but against using it as a substitute for justice and obedience.\n\nTHE NEW COVENANT IN JEREMIAH 31:31–34\nGod promised a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Judah, unlike the covenant broken after the Exodus. Its distinguishing features are: God's law written within the people and on their hearts; the relationship “I will be their God, and they shall be my people”; knowledge of God; and forgiveness of sin. This is not the rainbow covenant with Noah or the sign of circumcision with Abraham. Jeremiah 18 uses a potter and clay: the potter reshapes a spoiled vessel, illustrating God's authority and the importance of a nation's response to warning.\n\nEZEKIEL'S CALL AND SYMBOLIC ACTIONS\nEzekiel was among the exiles by the river Chebar when he received visions of God's glory (Ezekiel 1). In Ezekiel 2–3 he was called to speak to a rebellious house and was given a scroll to eat; it tasted sweet as honey. He was appointed a watchman responsible for warning people (Ezekiel 3:16–21; 33:1–9). The watchman image concerns the duty to deliver God's warning; it does not mean that the prophet can force people to obey.\nEzekiel 18 challenges the proverb “The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.” The chapter gives examples of a righteous father, a wicked son and a righteous grandson. Its central teaching is that each person is accountable for their own conduct, and someone who turns away from wrongdoing can live. Ezekiel 36:26 promises a new heart and a new spirit. Ezekiel 37:1–14 presents a valley of dry bones: bones come together, receive flesh and breath, and become living people. God explains that the vision signifies the restoration of the house of Israel from hopelessness. It is not merely a story about a cemetery.\n\nJONAH — THE ENTIRE SEQUENCE (JONAH 1–4)\nGod told Jonah to go to Nineveh and speak against its wickedness. Jonah instead went down to Joppa and boarded a ship bound for Tarshish. A great storm arose; the sailors cast lots and the lot fell on Jonah. At his request they threw him into the sea, and the sea became calm. A great fish swallowed Jonah, and he was inside it for three days and three nights (Jonah 1:17). He prayed, was released onto dry land and received the command again. Jonah proclaimed that Nineveh would be overthrown in 40 days (Jonah 3:4). From the king downwards, the people fasted, wore sackcloth and turned from evil; God did not bring the threatened destruction.\nJonah was angry at God's mercy. God appointed a plant to shade him, then a worm that attacked it, and a scorching east wind. Jonah cared deeply about the plant, and God used the incident to challenge his lack of compassion for Nineveh's inhabitants (Jonah 4). The plant was not the reason Nineveh repented.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — PROPHETS AND IMAGES\nExample (vii): What objection did Jeremiah raise when called?\nSolution: Jeremiah 1:6 says he did not know how to speak because he was young. Answer: His youth and inability to speak.\nExample (viii): What did Ezekiel eat in his call vision?\nSolution: Ezekiel 3:1–3 describes a scroll that tasted sweet as honey. Answer: A scroll.\nExample (ix): What did the dry bones signify?\nSolution: Ezekiel 37 explains them as the house of Israel and God's promise to restore them. Answer: Israel's restoration from hopelessness.\nExample (x): Where did Jonah board a ship while fleeing his commission?\nSolution: Jonah 1:3 says Joppa. Answer: Joppa.\nExample (xi): How long was Jonah inside the great fish?\nSolution: Jonah 1:17 says three days and three nights. Answer: Three days and three nights.\nExample (xii): How many days did Jonah announce before Nineveh's destruction?\nSolution: Jonah 3:4 gives 40 days. Answer: Forty days.\nExample (xiii): What was Jeremiah's new covenant to be written on?\nSolution: Jeremiah 31:33 says the people's hearts. Answer: Their hearts.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nCompare Hosea's return to God, Jeremiah's inward covenant and Ezekiel's individual responsibility. Explain the potter-and-clay image and the dry-bones vision. Retell Jonah's journey from Joppa to Nineveh, including the numbers three and 40. Explain the lesson of Jonah's plant.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDefine repentance and restoration. Explain personal responsibility in Ezekiel 18. Compare Jeremiah's new covenant with Hosea's call to return.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nPractise the passages using the linked JAMB questions.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nRepentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."},"old":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"Repentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."}},{"id":"exile-return","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"EXILE, FAITHFULNESS AND THE RETURN TO JERUSALEM — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nExile is the forced removal of people from their homeland. Judah's exile followed the Babylonian conquest of Jerusalem. Biblical writers connect the disaster with persistent disobedience and injustice. The return from exile later involved rebuilding community life and worship.\n\n1. THE FALL OF JERUSALEM (2 Kings 25; Jeremiah 39)\nBabylon captured Jerusalem during the reign of Zedekiah. He fled, was caught in the plains of Jericho, and was taken to Riblah for judgement. His sons were killed before him, and his eyes were put out. Remember the distinction: he was captured near Jericho but judged at Riblah. Jerusalem and its Temple were destroyed, and many inhabitants were deported; some poorer people remained to work the land.\n\n2. JEREMIAH AND EZEKIEL: DIFFERENT CALLS\nJeremiah 1 describes God's call of Jeremiah before his birth, his objection that he was young, and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel 2–3 describes a commission to speak to a rebellious people, whether or not they listen. The prophets both warned God's people, but their calling stories must not be mixed up.\n\n3. DANIEL: FAITHFULNESS IN A FOREIGN LAND\nDaniel and his companions faced pressure to compromise their worship.\nDaniel 3 — Nebuchadnezzar ordered worship of a golden image. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused and survived the fiery furnace.\nDaniel 6 — under Darius, Daniel's habit of praying became the basis of an accusation. He was thrown into a den of lions and survived.\nThe furnace and lions' den involved different rulers, accusations and people.\n\n4. THE RETURN AND REBUILDING\nAfter Babylon fell to Persia, Cyrus permitted exiles to return and rebuild the Temple (Ezra 1). Zerubbabel is associated with the rebuilt Temple; Ezra with teaching the law and reform; Nehemiah with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls and organising the community. The Temple and the city walls are not the same project.\nOpposition and discouragement occurred during rebuilding, but the community was called to perseverance and covenant obedience.\n\n5. WHAT THE EVENTS TEACH\nFaithfulness is possible even under pressure. Courage does not always mean the absence of danger. Rebuilding after disaster requires leadership, cooperation, worship and just conduct.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — THE HISTORICAL SEQUENCE\nJudah was conquered by Babylon, and Jerusalem's Temple was destroyed. Exile meant loss of home, political independence and familiar worship arrangements. After Persia conquered Babylon, Cyrus allowed some exiles to return. Not everyone returned at once; rebuilding happened in stages and involved opposition and setbacks.\nSequence: Jerusalem's fall → Babylonian exile → Persian rule → return under Cyrus' permission → rebuilding the Temple → later reforms and rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls.\n\nDANIEL AND HIS COMPANIONS — THREE DIFFERENT TESTS\nDaniel 1: Daniel and his companions declined the royal food and wine because they wished to avoid defilement. They were granted permission for a test involving vegetables and water; the story says they appeared healthy.\nDaniel 3: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused to bow to Nebuchadnezzar's golden image and survived the fiery furnace.\nDaniel 6: Daniel continued praying despite a decree under Darius and survived the lions' den.\nEach event has a different pressure, ruler or response. Do not answer a furnace question with the lions' den story.\n\nTHE REBUILDERS AND THEIR TASKS\nZerubbabel was associated with the return and rebuilding of the Temple (Ezra 3–6). Ezra was a priest and scribe who taught the law and led religious reform (Ezra 7–10). Nehemiah, who served the Persian king as cupbearer, led the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls (Nehemiah 1–6). Temple rebuilding and wall rebuilding are separate tasks. Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the rebuilding of the Temple.\n\nFAITHFULNESS AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP\nDaniel's stories emphasise loyalty under pressure. The return narratives show that restoring a community involves more than construction: people must cooperate, learn the law and act justly. Nehemiah organised workers and responded to threats while the wall was being rebuilt.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — PEOPLE AND PROJECTS\nWhen the question names the Temple, consider Zerubbabel and the prophetic encouragement of Haggai and Zechariah. When it names teaching the law, consider Ezra. When it names Jerusalem's walls, consider Nehemiah. When it names the fiery furnace, consider Nebuchadnezzar and Daniel's three companions.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Which Persian ruler permitted exiles to return and rebuild the Temple?\nSolution: Ezra 1 names Cyrus. Answer: Cyrus.\nExample (v): Which leader organised the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls?\nSolution: Nehemiah 2–6 records his leadership of the work. Answer: Nehemiah.\nExample (vi): What distinguished Daniel's test in Daniel 1 from the fiery furnace?\nSolution: Daniel 1 concerns food and defilement; Daniel 3 concerns refusal to worship an image. Answer: They involved different acts of faithfulness and different challenges.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nZedekiah was captured near Jericho but judged at Riblah. The three companions were thrown into the furnace; Daniel was thrown into the lions' den. Do not confuse Cyrus with Nebuchadnezzar or Darius.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where was Zedekiah captured, and where was he judged?\nSolution: Jeremiah 39 and 2 Kings 25 distinguish the plains of Jericho from Riblah. Answer: Captured near Jericho; judged at Riblah.\n\nExample (ii): Which king ordered the worship of the golden image in Daniel 3?\nSolution: Daniel 3 identifies Nebuchadnezzar. Answer: Nebuchadnezzar.\n\nExample (iii): Who is especially associated with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls?\nSolution: Nehemiah led the wall-building project described in the book bearing his name. Answer: Nehemiah.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — JERUSALEM'S FALL AND THE EXILE\nBabylon's King Nebuchadnezzar attacked Judah. An earlier deportation included King Jehoiachin, who surrendered and was taken to Babylon with leading citizens and craftsmen (2 Kings 24:10–17). Nebuchadnezzar installed Mattaniah, renamed Zedekiah, as king. Zedekiah rebelled. In the ninth year of his reign Babylon besieged Jerusalem; the siege lasted into his eleventh year (2 Kings 25:1–3). When the city wall was breached, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho and taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before his eyes and he was blinded (2 Kings 25:4–7).\nNebuzaradan, captain of the guard, burned the Temple, royal palace and houses, broke down Jerusalem's walls and carried people into exile (2 Kings 25:8–12). Some of the poorest people remained to work vineyards and fields. Gedaliah was appointed governor over those who remained but was later assassinated (2 Kings 25:22–26). Distinguish Nebuchadnezzar the king from Nebuzaradan the official who supervised destruction.\n\nDANIEL 1 — NAMES AND THE TEN-DAY TEST\nDaniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah were among the youths chosen for royal training in Babylon. Their Babylonian names were Belteshazzar, Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego respectively (Daniel 1:6–7). Daniel asked not to defile himself with royal food and wine. The official feared for his safety, so Daniel proposed a test of vegetables and water for ten days. At the end they appeared healthier than those eating the royal food. God gave them knowledge and skill; Daniel received understanding of visions and dreams. Ten days is the food test, not the duration of the exile.\n\nDANIEL 2 AND 3 — THE KING'S DREAM AND GOLDEN IMAGE\nNebuchadnezzar dreamed of a great statue with a head of gold, chest and arms of silver, belly and thighs of bronze, legs of iron, and feet partly of iron and clay. A stone struck the statue and destroyed it; Daniel explained the dream as a succession of kingdoms and God's enduring kingdom (Daniel 2). Daniel credited God with revealing the mystery.\nIn Daniel 3 Nebuchadnezzar set up a golden image 60 cubits high and six cubits wide on the plain of Dura. People were ordered to bow when musical instruments sounded. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused. The furnace was heated seven times hotter than usual; the men were bound and thrown in. The king saw four figures walking unharmed in the fire. The three emerged without the smell of fire on them. Daniel himself is not named among those thrown into the furnace.\n\nDANIEL 5 AND 6 — BELSHAZZAR AND DARIUS\nAt Belshazzar's feast, vessels taken from the Jerusalem Temple were used while guests praised idols. A hand wrote on the wall; Daniel interpreted the writing “Mene, Mene, Tekel, Parsin” as judgement on Belshazzar's kingdom (Daniel 5). That night Belshazzar was killed, and Darius the Mede received the kingdom in the narrative.\nIn Daniel 6 officials persuaded Darius to issue a decree that petitions must be addressed only to the king for 30 days. Daniel continued praying three times daily with his windows open toward Jerusalem. He was thrown into the lions' den, but God sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths. Darius was distressed by the decree and later honoured Daniel's God. Thirty days is the decree's duration; three times daily is Daniel's prayer practice.\n\nCYRUS, ZERUBBABEL, EZRA AND NEHEMIAH — DO NOT CONFUSE THEIR TASKS\nEzra 1:1–4 records Cyrus king of Persia permitting the return and the rebuilding of the LORD's house in Jerusalem. Cyrus returned Temple vessels taken by Nebuchadnezzar. Sheshbazzar is named in Ezra 1:8–11 in connection with receiving the vessels; Zerubbabel led the rebuilding community in the accounts of Ezra 3–6. The altar was rebuilt and the Temple foundation laid, but opposition delayed work. Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the people; the Temple was completed in the sixth year of Darius (Ezra 6:15).\nEzra was a priest and scribe skilled in the law of Moses (Ezra 7:6). Under Artaxerxes he travelled to Jerusalem to teach God's law and address covenant unfaithfulness. Nehemiah served Artaxerxes as cupbearer (Nehemiah 1–2). Hearing that Jerusalem's walls were broken, he prayed and sought permission to rebuild. Sanballat and Tobiah opposed the work, later joined by Geshem. Workers repaired sections of the wall and guarded against attack. Nehemiah 6:15 says the wall was completed in 52 days. The completion of the wall is not the same as the completion of the Temple.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — CHRONOLOGY AND NUMBERS\nExample (vii): Who blinded Zedekiah after his capture?\nSolution: 2 Kings 25 describes the judgement carried out under the Babylonian king after Zedekiah was brought to Riblah. Answer: Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon.\nExample (viii): How many days did Daniel's food test last?\nSolution: Daniel 1:12 says ten days. Answer: Ten days.\nExample (ix): What were Daniel's three companions called in Babylon?\nSolution: Daniel 1:7 names Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. Answer: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego.\nExample (x): How often did Daniel pray despite the decree?\nSolution: Daniel 6:10 says three times daily. Answer: Three times a day.\nExample (xi): For how long did the decree against prayer last?\nSolution: Daniel 6:7 specifies 30 days. Answer: Thirty days.\nExample (xii): In how many days was Jerusalem's wall completed under Nehemiah?\nSolution: Nehemiah 6:15 records 52 days. Answer: Fifty-two days.\nExample (xiii): Which prophets encouraged rebuilding the Temple?\nSolution: Ezra 5:1 names Haggai and Zechariah. Answer: Haggai and Zechariah.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nExplain the differences between Jehoiachin and Zedekiah. Distinguish Nebuchadnezzar from Nebuzaradan. Memorise the Hebrew and Babylonian names of Daniel's companions. Compare the ten-day food test, 30-day decree and 52-day wall rebuilding. Explain the separate tasks of Cyrus, Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDistinguish Daniel 3 from Daniel 6. Explain the difference between Temple rebuilding and wall rebuilding. State one lesson from the exile.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked past questions to practise people, places and the sequence of events.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nExile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."},"old":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"Exile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."}}];
